**Classmate 2:**
Sure. If we declare the `toString` method as package-private, it means the method will only be accessible within the same package. This would prevent other packages from calling the `toString` method on our objects, which could lead to unexpected behavior or errors, especially when using standard Java libraries or frameworks that rely on the `toString` method being `public`.

For example, consider the following scenario:

```java
package object_orientation2;

class Person {
    private String id;

    public Person(String id) {
        this.id = id;
    }

    // Package-private toString method
    String toString() {
