Certainly! Let's focus on implementing the `insertLast(E info)` method for the `MyBasicLinkedList<E>` class. This method will insert a new node containing `info` at the end of the list. We will pay special attention to the case where the list is empty and try to use methods already implemented.

### Updated MyBasicLinkedList<E> Class with insertLast Method

```java
public class MyBasicLinkedList<E> {
    private Node<E> head;
    private int size;

    public MyBasicLinkedList() {
        head = null;
        size = 0;
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return head == null;
    }

    public void insert(E info) {
        Node<E> newNode = new Node<>(info, head);
        head = newNode;
        size++;
    }

    public E extract() {
        if (isEmpty()) {
            return null;
        }
        Node<E> temp = head;
        head = head.getNext();
        size--;
        return temp.getInfo();
    }

    public void insert(E info, Node<E> previous) {
        if (previous == null) {
            insert(info);
        } else {
            Node<E> newNode = new Node<>(info, previous.getNext());
            previous.setNext(newNode);
            size++;
        }
    }

    public E extract(Node<E> previous) {
        if (previous == null || previous.getNext() == null) {
            return null;
        }
        Node<E> temp = previous.getNext();
        previous.setNext(temp.getNext());
        size--;
        return temp.getInfo();
    }

    public int size() {
        return size;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        Node<E> current = head;
        while (current != null) {
            sb.append(current);
            if (current.getNext() != null) {
                sb.append(" -> ");
            }
            current = current.getNext();
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }

    public void print() {
        System.out.println(this);
    }

    public Node<E> searchNode(E info) {
        Node<E> current = head;
        while (current != null) {
            if (current.getInfo().equals(info)) {
                return current;
            }
            current = current.getNext();
        }
        return null;
    }

    public Node<E> searchNode(int n) {
        if (n < 0 || n >= size) {
            return null;
        }
        Node<E> current = head;
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            current = current.getNext();
        }
        return current;
    }

    public Node<E> searchLastNode() {
        if (isEmpty()) {
            return null;
        }
        Node<E> current = head;
        while (current.getNext() != null) {
            current = current.getNext();
        }
        return current;
    }

    public int search(E info) {
        Node<E> current = head;
        int index = 0;
        while (current != null) {
            if (current.getInfo().equals(info)) {
                return index;
            }
            current = current.getNext();
            index++;
        }
        return -1;
    }

    // New insertLast method
    public void insertLast(E info) {
        if (isEmpty()) {
            insert(info);
        } else {
            Node<E> lastNode = searchLastNode();
            insert(info, lastNode);
        }
    }
}
```

### Explanation of insertLast Method

1. **Check if the list is empty**: If the list is empty, we use the `insert(info)` method to insert the new node at the beginning of the list.
2. **Find the last node**: If the list is not empty, we use the `searchLastNode()` method to find the last node in the list.
3. **Insert the new node after the last node**: We use the `insert(info, lastNode)` method to insert the new node after the last node.

### Testing the insertLast Method

#### MyBasicLinkedListTest Class

```java
public class MyBasicLinkedListTest {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyBasicLinkedList<String> list = new MyBasicLinkedList<>();

        // Test insertLast on an empty list
        list.insertLast("a");
        System.out.println(list); // Output: a

        // Test insertLast on a non-empty list
        list.insertLast("b");
        list.insertLast("c");
        System.out.println(list); // Output: a -> b -> c

        // Test other methods to ensure they still work correctly
        System.out.println(list.extract()); // Output: a
        System.out.println(list); // Output: b -> c

        list.insertLast("d");
        System.out.println(list); // Output: b -> c -> d

        list.print(); // Output: b -> c -> d
    }
}
```

### Summary

- **insertLast Method**: This method inserts a new node containing `info` at the end of the list. It handles the case where the list is empty by using the `insert(info)` method and finds the last node using the `searchLastNode()` method.
- **Testing**: The `MyBasicLinkedListTest` class includes tests for the `insertLast` method to ensure it works correctly for both empty and non-empty lists.

