If you have different setup requirements for different test cases, you can handle this in several ways:

1. **Conditional Logic in `@BeforeEach`**: Use flags or conditions within the `@BeforeEach` method to determine how to set up the test environment for each test case.

2. **Separate `@BeforeEach` Methods**: Use multiple `@BeforeEach` methods with different names and call them explicitly within the test methods.

3. **`@Nested` Test Classes**: Group related tests into nested classes, each with its own `@BeforeEach` method.

### Example 1: Conditional Logic in `@BeforeEach`

```java
package Lab6;

import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.BeforeEach;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;

class PointTest {

    private Point p;
    private boolean specialSetup;

    @BeforeEach
    void setUp() {
        if (specialSetup) {
            p = new Point(5.0, 6.0);
        } else {
            p = new Point(3.0, 4.0);
        }
    }

    @Test
    void testDefaultGetX() {
        specialSetup = false;
        setUp();
        assertEquals(3.0, p.getX());
    }

    @Test
    void testSpecialGetX() {
        specialSetup = true;
        setUp();
        assertEquals(5.0, p.getX());
    }
}
```

### Example 2: Separate `@BeforeEach` Methods

```java
package Lab6;

import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.BeforeEach;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;

class PointTest {

    private Point p;

    @BeforeEach
    void setUpDefault() {
        p = new Point(3.0, 4.0);
    }

    @BeforeEach
    void setUpSpecial() {
        p = new Point(5.0, 6.0);
    }

    @Test
    void testDefaultGetX() {
        setUpDefault();
        assertEquals(3.0, p.getX());
    }

    @Test
    void testSpecialGetX() {
        setUpSpecial();
        assertEquals(5.0, p.getX());
    }
}
```

### Example 3: `@Nested` Test Classes

```java
package Lab6;

import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.BeforeEach;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Nested;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;

class PointTest {

    @Nested
    class DefaultSetupTests {
        private Point p;

        @BeforeEach
        void setUp() {
            p = new Point(3.0, 4.0);
        }

        @Test
        void testGetX() {
            assertEquals(3.0, p.getX());
        }

        @Test
        void testGetY() {
            assertEquals(4.0, p.getY());
        }
    }

    @Nested
    class SpecialSetupTests {
        private Point p;

        @BeforeEach
        void setUp() {
            p = new Point(5.0, 6.0);
        }

        @Test
        void testGetX() {
            assertEquals(5.0, p.getX());
        }

        @Test
        void testGetY() {
            assertEquals(6.0, p.getY());
        }
    }
}
```

### Explanation:

- **Conditional Logic**: Use a flag (`specialSetup`) to determine the setup within the `@BeforeEach` method. This allows you to customize the setup for each test case.
- **Separate `@BeforeEach` Methods**: Define multiple setup methods and call them explicitly within the test methods. This approach provides flexibility but requires manual invocation of the setup methods.
- **`@Nested` Test Classes**: Group related tests into nested classes, each with its own `@BeforeEach` method. This approach keeps the setup logic organized and ensures that each group of tests has its own setup.

### Conclusion:

If you have different setup requirements for different test cases, you can use conditional logic within the `@BeforeEach` method, define separate setup methods, or organize tests into nested classes. Each approach has its advantages, and you can choose the one that best fits your needs.

---

If you have any more questions or need further clarification, feel free to ask!

