import org.junit.jupiter.api.BeforeEach;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;

class PointTest {

    private Point p;
    private boolean specialSetup;

    @BeforeEach
    void setUp() {
        if (specialSetup) {
            p = new Point(5.0, 6.0);
        } else {
            p = new Point(3.0, 4.0);
        }
    }

    @Test
    void testGetX() {
        specialSetup = false;
        setUp();
        assertEquals(3.0, p.getX());
    }

    @Test
    void testSpecialGetX() {
        specialSetup = true;
        setUp();
        assertEquals(5.0, p.getX());
    }
}
```

### Example with `@Nested` Test Classes:

```java
package Lab6;

import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.BeforeEach;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Nested;
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;

class PointTest {

    @Nested
    class DefaultSetupTests {
        private Point p;

        @BeforeEach
        void setUp() {
            p = new Point(3.0, 4.0);
        }

        @Test
        void testGetX() {
            assertEquals(3.0, p.getX());
        }

        @Test
        void testGetY() {
            assertEquals(4.0, p.getY());
        }
    }

    @Nested
    class SpecialSetupTests {
        private Point p;

        @BeforeEach
        void setUp() {
            p = new Point(5.0, 6.0);
        }

        @Test
        void testGetX() {
            assertEquals(5.0, p.getX());
        }

        @Test
        void testGetY() {
            assertEquals(6.0, p.getY());
        }
    }
}
```

### Summary:

- **Method Name**: The method annotated with `@BeforeEach` can have any name. `setUp()` is a common convention but not mandatory.
- **Multiple Setups**: If you need different setups for different tests, you can use conditional logic within the `@BeforeEach` method or use `@Nested` test classes to group tests with different setups.
- **JUnit 5**: Use `@BeforeEach` for JUnit 5. `@Before` is from JUnit 4 and should not be used in JUnit 5.

### Conclusion:

`@BeforeEach` is a powerful tool for setting up test environments and ensuring that each test method starts with a fresh state. It is not mandatory but highly recommended for avoiding common mistakes like code duplication and test interference. You can use any method name with `@BeforeEach`, and if you need different setups, you can use conditional logic or `@Nested` test classes.

