To create an array of objects of different classes (`Person` and `Student` in this case) and print them using a loop, you can take advantage of polymorphism. Since `Student` is a subclass of `Person`, you can create an array of `Person` and store both `Person` and `Student` objects in it.

Here is an example of how you can do this:

```java
package OOPlab2;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Create an array of Person objects
        Person[] people = new Person[3];

        // Add Person and Student objects to the array
        people[0] = new Person("12345678A");
        people[1] = new Student("87654321B", "20210001");
        people[2] = new Student("11223344C", "20210002");

        // Loop through the array and print each object
        for (Person person : people) {
            System.out.println(person.toString());
        }
    }
}
```

In this example:
- We create an array of `Person` objects called `people`.
- We add instances of `Person` and `Student` to the array.
- We use an enhanced for loop (`for (Person person : people)`) to iterate through the array and print each object using the `toString` method.

Here is the complete code for the `Person` and `Student` classes, along with the `Main` class:

```java
package OOPlab2;

public class Person {
    // Attributes
    public String dni;

    // Constructor
    Person(String dni) {
        this.dni = dni;
    }

    // Methods
    @Override
    public String toString() {
        // Ensure the dni is not null and has at least 2 characters
        if (dni != null && dni.length() > 1) {
            return "DNI: " + dni.substring(0, dni.length() - 1) + "-" + dni.charAt(dni.length() - 1);
        } else {
            return "DNI: " + dni; // Fallback if dni is too short or null
        }
    }
}

package OOPlab2;

public class Student extends Person {
    // Attributes
    public String nia;

    // Constructor
    Student(String dni, String nia) {
        super(dni); // Correctly call the constructor of the superclass
        this.nia = nia;
    }

    // Methods
    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return super.toString() + ", NIA: " + this.nia;
    }
}

package OOPlab2;

