### 1. Node<E> Class

#### Does Node<E> class extend any class? Does it implement any interface?
No, the `Node<E>` class does not extend any class or implement any interface.

#### Attributes
The class has the following attributes:
- `info` of type `E` (generic type)
- `next` of type `Node<E>`

Both attributes should be private.

#### Constructors
We will define two constructors:
1. A no-argument constructor that initializes the attributes to `null`.
2. A constructor that initializes both attributes.

#### Methods
We will implement the following methods:
- Getters and setters for `info` and `next`.
- Override the `toString()` method to return the string representation of `info`.

Here is the implementation of the `Node<E>` class:

```java
public class Node<E> {
    private E info;
    private Node<E> next;

    // No-argument constructor
    public Node() {
        this(null, null);
    }

    // Constructor with arguments
    public Node(E info) {
        this(info, null);
    }

    // Constructor with all attributes
    public Node(E info, Node<E> next) {
        this.info = info;
        this.next = next;
    }

    // Getter for info
    public E getInfo() {
        return info;
    }

    // Setter for info
    public void setInfo(E info) {
        this.info = info;
    }

    // Getter for next
    public Node<E> getNext() {
        return next;
    }

    // Setter for next
    public void setNext(Node<E> next) {
        this.next = next;
    }

    // Override toString method
    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return info != null ? info.toString() : "null";
    }
}
```

