To ensure that `MAXLOAN` is initialized only once and remains constant throughout the execution of the program, you should use the `static` and `final` modifiers together. 

Here is how you can define `MAXLOAN`:

```java
public class Loan {
    // Constant variable
    public static final int MAXLOAN = 10000;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Accessing the constant variable
        System.out.println("Max Loan: " + Loan.MAXLOAN);
    }
}
```

In this example:
- `static` ensures that `MAXLOAN` is a class variable, meaning it is shared among all instances of the class.
- `final` ensures that `MAXLOAN` can only be initialized once and its value cannot be changed.

So, the correct modifiers to use are `static` and `final`.

The correct term for attributes or variables that have the same value for all objects in the class is:

*class attributes or class variables*

In Java, these are defined using the `static` keyword. Here is an example:

```java
public class ExampleClass {
    // This is a class variable
    private static int sharedValue = 10;

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Accessing the class variable
        System.out.println("Shared Value: " + ExampleClass.sharedValue);
    }
}
```

In this example, `sharedValue` is a class variable because it is declared with the `static` keyword, meaning it is shared among all instances of `ExampleClass`.

*Remember*: Class variables are also known as static variables.

"Set" methods allow:

*changing the value of attributes*

These methods are also known as *mutators*. They are used to modify the value of private attributes from outside the class. Here is an example:

```java
public class ExampleClass {
    // Private attribute
    private int value;

    // Setter method
    public void setValue(int value) {
        this.value = value;
    }

    // Getter method
    public int getValue() {
        return value;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ExampleClass obj = new ExampleClass();
        // Using the setter method to change the value
        obj.setValue(10);
        // Using the getter method to obtain the value
        System.out.println("Value: " + obj.getValue());
    }
}
```

In this example, the `setValue` method is used to change the value of the `value` attribute, while the `getValue` method is used to obtain the value of the `value` attribute.

"Get" and "Set" methods:

*are public, in general*

These methods are used to provide controlled access to the private attributes of a class. By making these methods public, you allow other classes to read and modify the values of private attributes in a controlled manner. Here is an example:

```java
public class ExampleClass {
    // Private attribute
    private int value;

    // Public setter method
    public void setValue(int value) {
        this.value = value;
    }

    // Public getter method
    public int getValue() {
        return value;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ExampleClass obj = new ExampleClass();
        // Using the setter method to change the value
        obj.setValue(10);
        // Using the getter method to obtain the value
        System.out.println("Value: " + obj.getValue());
    }
}
```

In this example, both the `setValue` and `getValue` methods are public, allowing other classes to access and modify the `value` attribute in a controlled way.

