A **node** in Java is a fundamental building block used in various data structures such as linked lists, trees, and graphs. It typically contains data and references (or links) to other nodes. Let's look at a few examples of node classes in Java.

### Example of a Node in a Singly Linked List

```java
public class Node<E> {
    private E info; // Data stored in the node
    private Node<E> next; // Reference to the next node

    // Constructor to initialize the node with data
    public Node(E info) {
        this.info = info;
        this.next = null;
    }

    // Getter for the next node
    public Node<E> getNext() {
        return next;
    }

    // Setter for the next node
    public void setNext(Node<E> next) {
        this.next = next;
    }

    // Getter for the data
    public E getInfo() {
        return info;
    }

    // Setter for the data
    public void setInfo(E info) {
        this.info = info;
    }
}
```

### Example of a Node in a Doubly Linked List

```java
public class DLNode<E> {
    private E info; // Data stored in the node
    private DLNode<E> prev; // Reference to the previous node
    private DLNode<E> next; // Reference to the next node

    // Constructor to initialize the node with data
    public DLNode(E info) {
        this.info = info;
        this.prev = null;
        this.next = null;
    }

    // Constructor to initialize the node with data and references
    public DLNode(E info, DLNode<E> prev, DLNode<E> next) {
        this.info = info;
        this.prev = prev;
        this.next = next;
    }

    // Getter for the next node
    public DLNode<E> getNext() {
        return next;
    }

    // Setter for the next node
    public void setNext(DLNode<E> next) {
        this.next = next;
    }

    // Getter for the previous node
    public DLNode<E> getPrev() {
        return prev;
    }

    // Setter for the previous node
    public void setPrev(DLNode<E> prev) {
        this.prev = prev;
    }

    // Getter for the data
    public E getInfo() {
        return info;
    }

    // Setter for the data
    public void setInfo(E info) {
        this.info = info;
    }
}
```

### Example of a Node in a Binary Tree

```java
public class LBNode<E> {
    private E info; // Data stored in the node
    private LBNode<E> left; // Reference to the left child node
    private LBNode<E> right; // Reference to the right child node

    // Constructor to initialize the node with data
    public LBNode(E info) {
        this.info = info;
        this.left = null;
        this.right = null;
    }

    // Constructor to initialize the node with data and child nodes
    public LBNode(E info, LBNode<E> left, LBNode<E> right) {
        this.info = info;
        this.left = left;
        this.right = right;
    }

    // Getter for the left child node
    public LBNode<E> getLeft() {
        return left;
    }

    // Setter for the left child node
    public void setLeft(LBNode<E> left) {
        this.left = left;
    }

    // Getter for the right child node
    public LBNode<E> getRight() {
        return right;
    }

    // Setter for the right child node
    public void setRight(LBNode<E> right) {
        this.right = right;
    }

    // Getter for the data
    public E getInfo() {
        return info;
    }

    // Setter for the data
    public void setInfo(E info) {
        this.info = info;
    }
}
```

### Summary

- **Node**: A basic unit of a data structure that contains data and references to other nodes.
- **Singly Linked List Node**: Contains data and a reference to the next node.
- **Doubly Linked List Node**: Contains data and references to both the previous and next nodes.
- **Binary Tree Node**: Contains data and references to the left and right child nodes.

Nodes are essential for building complex data structures and enable efficient data manipulation and traversal.

