I see that you are trying to ensure that the points provided form a rectangle in the constructor of the `Rectangle` class. Here is the corrected and complete implementation of the `Rectangle` class along with the `Point` and `Quadrilateral` classes.

### `Point` Class

```java
public class Point {
    private double x;
    private double y;

    public Point(double x, double y) {
        this.x = x;
        this.y = y;
    }

    public double getX() {
        return x;
    }

    public double getY() {
        return y;
    }

    public double distance(Point other) {
        return Math.sqrt(Math.pow(this.x - other.x, 2) + Math.pow(this.y - other.y, 2));
    }
}
```

### `Quadrilateral` Class

```java
public abstract class Quadrilateral {
    protected String name;
    protected Point A, B, C, D;

    public Quadrilateral(String name, Point A, Point B, Point C, Point D) {
        this.name = name;
        this.A = A;
        this.B = B;
        this.C = C;
        this.D = D;
    }

    public abstract double area();

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return name + " with vertices: (" + A.getX() + ", " + A.getY() + "), (" + B.getX() + ", " + B.getY() + "), (" + C.getX() + ", " + C.getY() + "), (" + D.getX() + ", " + D.getY() + ")";
    }
}
```

### `Rectangle` Class

```java
public class Rectangle extends Quadrilateral {

    public Rectangle(String name, Point v1, Point v2, Point v3, Point v4) {
        super(name, v1, v2, v3, v4);
        if (!this.checkRectangle(v1, v2, v3, v4)) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("The given points do not form a rectangle.");
        }
    }

    private boolean checkRectangle(Point v1, Point v2, Point v3, Point v4) {
        double d1 = v1.distance(v2);
        double d2 = v2.distance(v3);
        double d3 = v3.distance(v4);
        double d4 = v4.distance(v1);
        double diag1 = v1.distance(v3);
        double diag2 = v2.distance(v4);

        return d1 == d3 && d2 == d4 && diag1 == diag2;
    }

    @Override
    public double area() {
        double width = A.distance(B);
        double height = B.distance(C);
        return width * height;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return super.toString();
    }
}
```

### Key Points

- **Constructor**: The constructor of the `Rectangle` class calls the `super` constructor of the `Quadrilateral` class and then checks if the given points form a rectangle using the `checkRectangle` method.
- **Check Rectangle**: The `checkRectangle` method ensures that opposite sides are equal and the diagonals are equal, which is a property of rectangles.
- **Area Calculation**: The `area` method calculates the area of the rectangle by multiplying the width and height.

