### `Point` Class Implementation:

```java
package object_orientation;

public class Point {
    private double x;
    private double y;

    public Point(double x, double y) {
        this.x = x;
        this.y = y;
    }

    public double getX() {
        return x;
    }

    public double getY() {
        return y;
    }

    public double distance() {
        return Math.sqrt(x * x + y * y);
    }

    public String quadrant() {
        if (x > 0 && y > 0) {
            return "First quadrant";
        } else if (x < 0 && y > 0) {
            return "Second quadrant";
        } else if (x < 0 && y < 0) {
            return "Third quadrant";
        } else if (x > 0 && y < 0) {
            return "Fourth quadrant";
        } else if (x == 0 && y == 0) {
            return "Origin";
        } else if (x == 0) {
            return "On the y-axis";
        } else {
            return "On the x-axis";
        }
    }

    public double distanceTo(Point other) {
        double dx = this.x - other.x;
        double dy = this.y - other.y;
        return Math.sqrt(dx * dx + dy * dy);
    }

    public Point nearest(Point[] points) {
        Point nearestPoint = points[0];
        double minDistance = this.distanceTo(points[0]);

        for (int i = 1; i < points.length; i++) {
            double distance = this.distanceTo(points[i]);
            if (distance < minDistance) {
                minDistance = distance;
                nearestPoint = points[i];
            }
        }

        return nearestPoint;
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return "(" + x + ", " + y + ")";
    }
}
```

### Summary:

- The `TestPoint` class now correctly initializes the `Point` objects in the `pointsArray`.
- The `Point` class includes methods to calculate the distance to the origin, determine the quadrant, calculate the distance to another point, and find the nearest point in an array of points.

